== 9. The Crosses of the Saints

The ninth device that Satan has to draw the soul to sin is by presenting to the soul the crosses, losses, reproaches, sorrows and sufferings that daily attend those who walk in the ways of holiness. Satan says, “Do you not see that there are none in the world so vexed, afflicted and tossed about as those who walk more carefully and more holily than their neighbors? They are a byword at home and a reproach abroad. Their miseries come in upon them like Job’s messengers, one on the heels of another, and there is no end to their sorrows and troubles. Therefore,” says Satan, “you had better walk in ways that are less troublesome and less afflicted, though they are more sinful. For who but a madman would spend his days in sorrow, vexation and affliction, when it may be avoided by walking in the ways that I set before him?”

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first remedy against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider that all the afflictions that attend the people of God are such as will turn to their profit and glorious advantage. They will reveal a filthiness and vileness in sin that the soul has never yet seen. A German divine said in his sickness, “In this disease#footnote[Gaspar Olevianus.] I have learned how great God is, and what the evil of sin is. I never truly knew what God was before, nor what sin meant, till now.” Afflictions are a crystal mirror in which the soul has the clearest sight of the ugly face of sin. In this mirror the soul comes to see that sin is only a bitter-sweet. Indeed, in this mirror the soul comes to see sin not only as an evil, but as the greatest evil in the world, an evil far worse than hell.

Again, afflictions will help in the#footnote[Isa 1:25; 27:8, 9.] mortifying and purging away of the saints’ sins. Afflictions are God’s furnace, by which he cleanses his people from their dross. Affliction is a fire to purge out#footnote[In times of peace our armor is rusty; in time of war it is bright.] our dross and to make virtue shine. It is a potion to carry away the body’s bad humors, better than all the #emph[Benedicta Medicamenta] \[blessed medicines\], as physicians call them. #emph[Aloes] kills worms, and colds and frosts kill vermin; so afflictions kill the corruptions that are in our hearts. The Jews kept their idols under all the thundering of the prophets; but after their captivity in Babylon, it is observed, no idols have been found among them.

Again, afflictions are sweet preservatives to keep the saints from sin, which is a greater evil than hell itself. As Job said, “Surely it is fitting to say to God,#footnote[Job 34:31, 32.] ‘I have borne chastisement; I will offend no more. Teach me what I cannot see; if I have done wrong, I will not do it again.’ Once I have spoken foolishly, indeed#footnote[Salt brine preserves from putrefaction, and salt marshes keep the sheep from the rot; so afflictions keep the saints from sin.] twice, but I will do so no more,” says Job. The burnt child dreads the fire. “Ah,” says the soul under the rod, “sin is only a bitter-sweet, and for the future I intend, by the strength of Christ, not to buy repentance at so dear a price.” The rabbis, to scare their students away from sin, used to tell them that sin made God’s head ache; and saints under the rod have found by woeful experience that sin makes not only their heads but their hearts ache too. Augustine, by wandering out of his way, escaped a man who lay in wait to harm him. If afflictions did not put us out of our way, we would many times meet with some sin or other that would harm our precious souls.

Again, afflictions will work the saints to be more fruitful in holiness. Heb 12:10, 11: “But God disciplines us for our profit, so that#footnote[The ball in the emblem says, #emph[Percussa Surgo:] the harder you beat me down in affliction, the higher I will bound in affection toward heaven and heavenly things.] we may share in His holiness.” The flowers smell sweetest after a shower. Vines bear better for bleeding. The walnut tree is most fruitful when it is most beaten. Saints spring up and thrive most inwardly when they are most outwardly afflicted. Afflictions are called by some the mother of virtue. Manasseh’s chain was more profitable to him than his crown. Luther could not understand some Scriptures till he was in affliction. The cross at the head of the child’s alphabet is no letter, and yet it taught him more than all the letters in the row. God’s house of#footnote[#emph[Schola crucis Schola lucis] \[The school of the cross is the school of light\].] correction is his school of instruction. All the stones that came about Stephen’s ears only knocked him closer to Christ the cornerstone. The waves only lifted Noah’s ark nearer to heaven, and the higher the waters rose, the nearer the ark was lifted up to heaven. Afflictions lift up the soul to richer, clearer and fuller enjoyments of God. Hos 2:14: “Behold, I will allure her and lead her to the wilderness, and speak to her tenderly,” or rather, as the Hebrew has it, I#footnote[ודברתי על־לבה Vedibbartignal-libbah.] will earnestly or vehemently speak to her heart. God makes afflictions only inlets to the soul’s sweeter and fuller enjoyment of his blessed self. When was it that Stephen saw the heavens open, and Christ standing at the right hand of God? It was when the stones were flying about#footnote[It is reported of Tiberius the emperor that, passing by a place where he saw a cross lying in the ground on a marble stone, he had the stone dug up and found a great deal of treasure under the cross. So many a precious saint has found much spiritual and heavenly treasure under the crosses he has met with.] his ears, and there was only a short step between him and eternity. And when did God appear in his glory to Jacob? It was in the day of his troubles, when the stones were his pillows, the ground his bed, the hedges his curtains, and the heavens his canopy. #emph[Then he saw the angels of God ascending and descending in their shining robes.] The plant in Nazianzen grows by cutting: being cut, it flourishes; it contends with the axe; it lives by dying, and by cutting it grows. So do saints by the afflictions that befall them. They gain more experience of the power of God supporting them, of the wisdom of God directing them, of the grace of God refreshing and cheering them, and of the goodness of God quieting them and stirring them up to a greater love for holiness, a greater delight in holiness, and a more vehement pursuit of holiness. I have read of a fountain that is cold at noonday and grows warm at midnight. So many a precious saint who is cold Godward, heavenward and holiness-ward in the day of prosperity grows warm Godward, heavenward and holiness-ward in the midnight of adversity.

Again, afflictions serve to keep the hearts of the saints humble and tender. Lam 3:19, 20: “I remember my affliction and wandering, the wormwood and the gall. My soul still remembers them#footnote[ותשיח from שוח] and is humbled within me,” or bowed down within me, as the original has it. So David, when he was under the rod, could say, “I have become mute; I do not open my mouth because of what You have done.” I have read of one who, whenever anything turned out prosperously, would read over the Lamentations of Jeremiah,#footnote[Gregory Nazianzen.] and that kept his heart tender, humble and low. Prosperity does no more to puff up the soul than adversity does to bow it down. The saints find this by experience, and therefore they can kiss and embrace the cross as others do the world’s crown.

Again, afflictions serve to bring the saints nearer to God, and to make them more importunate and earnest in prayer with God. “Before I was afflicted, I went astray;#footnote[Ps 119:67, 71. Hos 5:14, 15; 6:1, 2.] but now I keep Your word. It was good for me to be afflicted, that I might learn Your statutes.” “For I am like a lion to Ephraim and like a young lion to the house of#footnote[The more the most precious perfumes and the purest spices are beaten and bruised, the sweeter the scent and fragrance they send abroad; so do saints when they are afflicted.] Judah. I, even I, will tear them to pieces and then go away. I will carry them off where no one can rescue them. Then I will return to My place until they admit their guilt and seek My face; in their affliction they will earnestly seek Me.” And so they did: “Come,” they say, “let us return to the #smallcaps[Lord]. For He has torn us to pieces, but He will heal us; He has wounded us, but He will bind up our wounds. After two days He will revive us; on the third day He will raise us up, that we may live in His presence.” So when God had hedged up their way with thorns, then they say, “I will return#footnote[Hos 2:6, 7.] to my first husband, for then I was better off than now.” Ah, the joy, the peace, the comfort, the delight and contentment that attended us when we kept close communion with God call for our return to God. #emph[We will return to our first husband, for then we were better off than now.]

When Tiribazus, a Persian nobleman, was arrested, he drew his sword and defended himself; but when they told#footnote[Most men are like a top that will not spin unless you whip it, and the more you whip it, the better it spins; you know how to apply it. “Those who are in adversity,” says Luther, “understand Scripture better; but those who are in prosperity read it like a verse in Ovid.” Bees are killed with honey but revived with vinegar. The honey of prosperity kills our graces, but the vinegar of adversity revives our graces.] him that they had come to take him to the king, he yielded willingly. So, though a saint may at first hold out a little, yet when he remembers that afflictions are to carry him nearer to God, he yields and kisses the rod. Afflictions are like the thorn at the nightingale’s breast, which wakes her and sets her to her sweet and delightful singing.

Again, afflictions serve to revive and recover decayed graces. They inflame the love that has grown cold, and they quicken the faith that is decaying, and they put life into the hopes that are withering, and spirit into the joys and comforts that are languishing. Musk, says one, when it has lost its sweetness, recovers it if it is put into the sewer among filth; so afflictions recover and revive decayed graces. The more the saints are beaten with the hammer of afflictions, the more they are made the trumpets of God’s praises, and the more their graces are revived and quickened. Adversity abases the loveliness of the world that might entice us; it abates the lustiness of the flesh within that might incite us to folly and vanity; and it aids the spirit in its quarrel with those two, which does much to revive and recover decayed graces. Now suppose afflictions and troubles do attend the ways of holiness; yet seeing that they all work for the great profit and singular advantage of the saints, let no soul be so mad as to leave an afflicted way of holiness to walk in a smooth path of wickedness.

The second remedy against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider that all the afflictions that befall#footnote[The Christian soldier will always be master of the field. (#emph[Mori posse vinci non posse,] said Cyprian to Cornelius.) He may suffer death, but never conquest.] the saints reach only their worse part. They do not reach, they do not hurt, their noble part, their best part. All the arrows stick in the shield; they do not reach the conscience. 1 Pet 3:13: “Who can harm you#super[+] if you are zealous for what is good?” says the apostle; that is, none shall harm you#super[+]. They may afflict you#super[+] in this way and that, but they shall never harm you.

It was the speech of a heathen whom the tyrant commanded to be put into a mortar and beaten to pieces with an iron pestle. He cried out to his persecutors, “You beat only the vessel, the case, the husk of Anaxarchus; you do not beat me.” His body was to him only a case, a husk; he counted his soul himself, and that they could not reach. You are wise and know how to apply it. Socrates said of his enemies, “They may kill me, but they cannot hurt me.” So afflictions may kill us, but they cannot hurt us. They may take away my life, but they cannot take away my God, my Christ, my crown.

The third remedy against this device of Satan is seriously to consider that the afflictions that attend the saints in the ways of holiness are only short and momentary. “Sorrow may stay the night, but joy comes in the morning.”#footnote[None of God’s afflicted ones lack their (#emph[lucida intervalla]) intermissions, respites and breathing spaces under their short and momentary afflictions. When God’s hand is on your back, let your hand be on your mouth; for though the affliction is sharp, it will be only short.] This short storm will end in an everlasting calm; this short night will end in a glorious day that will never end. It is only a very short time between grace and glory, between our title to the crown and our wearing the crown, between our right to the heavenly inheritance and our possessing the heavenly inheritance. Fourteen thousand years to the Lord are only as one day. What is our life but a shadow, a bubble, a flower, a running messenger, a span, a dream, etc.? Indeed, the hand of the Lord rests on us for so small a while that Luther cannot find diminutives enough to make it small; for he calls it a very little little cross that we bear. The prophet in Isa 26:20 (#emph[to pikron mikron] \[the bitter is small\]) says the #emph[indignation] does not (#emph[transire]) pass, but (#emph[pertransire]) passes over. Its sharpness, shortness and suddenness are set forth by the labor of a woman, John 16:21. And that is a#footnote[John 16:21. Heb 10:36, 37.] sweet Scripture: “You#super[+] need to persevere, so that after you have done the will of God, you will receive what He has promised. For, ‘In just a little while, He who is coming will come and will not delay.’” (#emph[Tantillum, tantillum]#footnote[Ἔτι γὰρ μικρὸν ὅσον, ὅσον.] #emph[adhuc pusillum]): a little, little, little while. When Athanasius’s friends came to lament over him because of his misery and banishment, he said, “It is only a little cloud, and it will soon be gone.” #emph[It will be only as a day before God gives his]#footnote[#emph[Nubecula est, cito transibit.] Athanasius.] #emph[afflicted ones beauty for ashes, the oil of gladness for the spirit of heaviness;] before he turns all your#super[+] sighing into singing, all your lamentations into consolations, your sackcloth into silks, ashes into ointments, and your fasts into everlasting feasts, etc.

The fourth remedy against this device of Satan is seriously to consider that the afflictions that befall the saints are such as proceed from God’s dearest love. “Those I love, I rebuke#footnote[Augustine asks (#emph[Si amatur quomodo infirmatur]), “If he were loved, how did he come to be sick?” So wicked men are apt to say, because they do not know that corrections are pledges of our adoption and badges of our sonship. God had one Son without sin, but none without sorrow.] and discipline. Therefore be earnest and repent.” “Saints,” says God, “do not think that I hate you#super[+] because I rebuke you in this way.” He who escapes rebuke may suspect his adoption. God had one Son without corruption, but no son without correction. A gracious soul may look through the darkest cloud and see a God smiling on him. We must look through the anger of his correction to the sweetness of his face, as in a rainbow we see the beautiful image of the sun’s light in the midst of a dark and watery cloud. When Munster lay sick, and his friends asked him how he was and how he felt, he pointed to his sores and ulcers (of which he was full) and said, “These are God’s gems and jewels, with which he adorns his best friends, and to me they are more precious than all the gold and silver in the world.” A soul at its first conversion is only rough-cast; but God by afflictions squares it and fits it and fashions it for that glory above. This shows that they flow from precious love. Therefore the afflictions that attend the people of God should be no bar to holiness, and no motive to draw the soul to ways of wickedness.

The fifth remedy against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider that it is our duty and glory to measure afflictions not by the smart, but by the end. When Israel was sent out of#footnote[Exod 11.] Egypt, it was with #emph[gold] and #emph[earrings.] So the Jews were sent out of Babylon with #emph[gifts, jewels] and all necessary #emph[utensils.] Look more at the latter end of a#footnote[Ezra 1.] Christian than at the beginning of his affliction. Consider the patience of Job, and what end the Lord made with him. Do not look at Lazarus lying at the rich man’s#footnote[Afflictions are only our Father’s goldsmiths, who are working to add pearls to our crowns.] door, but lying in Abraham’s bosom. Do not look at the beginning of Joseph, who was so far from his dream that the sun and moon would bow to him, that for two years he was cast where he could see neither sun, moon, nor stars; but behold him at last made ruler over Egypt. Do not look at David when there was only a step between him and#footnote[Tiburtius saw #emph[paradise] when he walked on hot burning coals. Herodotus said of the Assyrians, “Let them drink nothing but wormwood all their life long; when they die, they will swim in honey.” You are wise and know how to apply it.] death, nor as he was envied by some and slighted and despised by others; but behold him seated on his royal throne, and dying in his bed of honor, with his son Solomon and all his glittering nobles about him. Afflictions are only a dark entry into your Father’s house; they are only a dirty lane to a royal palace. Now tell me, souls, is it not very great madness to shun the ways of holiness and to walk in the ways of wickedness because of the afflictions that attend the ways of holiness?

The sixth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is seriously to consider that God’s design in all the afflictions that befall the saints is only to test them, not to wrong them or ruin them, as ignorant souls are apt to think. “He knows the way I have taken; when He has tested me, I will come forth as gold,” says patient Job. So in Deut 8:2: “Remember that these forty years the #smallcaps[Lord] your God led you all the way in the wilderness, so that He might humble you and test you in order to know what was in your heart, whether or not#footnote[The King of Aracum in Scaliger tests the woman he means to marry by sweating: if they are sweet, he marries them; if not, he rejects them. You may easily make the application.] you would keep His commandments.” God afflicted them in this way so that he might make known to themselves and to others what was in their hearts. When fire is put to green wood, a great deal of watery stuff comes out that did not appear before. When the pond is empty, the mud, filth and toads come to light. The snow covers many a dunghill; so prosperity covers many a rotten heart. It is easy to wade in a warm bath, and every bird can sing on a sunny day, etc. Hard weather tests what health we have; afflictions test what sap we have, what grace we have. Withered leaves soon fall off in windy weather; rotten boughs quickly break under heavy weights, etc. You are wise and know how to apply it.

Afflictions are like pinching frosts#footnote[Dunghills raked send out a filthy stench, ointment a sweet perfume; this applies to sinners and saints under the rod.] that will search us out. Where we are most unsound, we will complain soonest, and where most corruptions lie, we will shrink most. We test metal by knocking; if it rings well, then we like it. So God tests his own by knocking, and if under the knocks they give a pleasant sound, God will turn their night into day, and their bitter into sweet, and their cross into a crown. And they will hear that voice: “Arise, shine, for … the glory of the #smallcaps[Lord] rises upon you,” and the favors of the Lord are flowing in on you.

The seventh and last #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider that the afflictions, wrath and misery that attend the ways of wickedness are far greater and heavier than those that attend the ways of holiness. Oh, the galling, cutting, lashing and gnawing of conscience that attend souls in a way of wickedness! “The wicked” (says Isaiah) “are like the storm-tossed sea, for it cannot be still, and its waves#footnote[Sin often makes men insensible of the wrath of the Almighty. Sin transforms many a man, as it were, into those bears in Pliny that could not be stirred by the sharpest prickles, or those fish in Aristotle that do not wake though spears are thrust into their sides.] churn up mire and muck. ‘There is no peace,’ says my God, ‘for the wicked.’” There are snares in all their mercies, and curses and crosses attend all their comforts, both at home and abroad. What is a fine suit of clothes with the plague in it? And what is a golden cup with poison at the bottom? Or what is a silk stocking with a broken leg in it? The curse of God, the wrath of God, the hatred of God and the fierce indignation of God always attend sinners walking in a way of wickedness. Turn to Deuteronomy 28 and read from verse 15 to the end of that chapter; and turn to Leviticus 26 and read from verse 14 to the end of that chapter. Then you will see how the curse of God haunts the wicked man (like an avenging Fury) in all his ways. In the city it attends him;#footnote[Sin brings in sorrow and sickness, etc. The rabbis say that when Adam tasted the forbidden fruit, his head ached.] in the country it hovers over him; coming in, it accompanies him; going out, it follows him; and in travel it is his companion. It fills his storehouse with strife, and mingles the wrath of God with his sweetest morsel. It is a moth in his wardrobe, a plague among his cattle, mildew in his field, rot among his sheep, and often makes the fruit of his body his greatest vexation and confusion. No solid joy, no lasting peace, no#footnote[Sirens are said to sing exquisitely while they live, but to roar horribly when they die; so do the wicked.] pure comfort attends sinners in their sinful ways. There is a sword of vengeance that every moment hangs over their heads by a thin thread; and what joy and contentment can attend such souls, if the eye of conscience is open just far enough to see the sword? Ah, the horrors and terrors, the tremblings and shakings, that attend their souls!
